WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.813
From makemore to GPT.

2
00:00:02.093 --> 00:00:08.045
Everything so far has been a model that reads a fixed, short context and predicts the next character.

3
00:00:08.325 --> 00:00:19.845
“Let’s build GPT” keeps that framing (a character-level, autoregressive language model) and changes one thing: how a position gathers information from the positions before it.

4
00:00:20.125 --> 00:00:24.648
In the MLP, the context was concatenated and pushed through fixed weights.

5
00:00:24.928 --> 00:00:38.069
In a transformer, each position computes a data-dependent weighted average of the earlier positions, called self-attention, and stacks of those averages, interleaved with per-position MLPs, make up the model.

6
00:00:38.349 --> 00:00:47.224
The lecture trains on “tiny Shakespeare,” about a megabyte of Shakespeare’s plays as one text file, with a vocabulary of the characters that appear in it.

7
00:00:47.504 --> 00:00:58.747
It starts from a bigram baseline, builds attention in four versions, assembles transformer blocks, scales up, and ends with a model of about ten million parameters (about 10.

8
00:00:59.027 --> 00:01:01.992
8 million if you count the layers in its gpt.

9
00:01:02.272 --> 00:01:07.648
py with a 65-character vocabulary) that writes plausible-looking pseudo-Shakespeare.

10
00:01:07.928 --> 00:01:17.315
The code it builds is published as ng-video-lecture (MIT), and Karpathy’s nanoGPT repository is the cleaned-up, faster version.

11
00:01:17.595 --> 00:01:21.776
The description recommends watching the makemore lectures first, which you have.

12
00:01:22.056 --> 00:01:27.219
The tensor operations that the video takes for granted are now the ones you’ve derived.
